@* Shared rank-medal rendering. The same #1 / #2 / #3 / blank shape was being inlined on
   Leaderboard (gold/silver/bronze chain) and Challenge (gold only), with the emoji glyph
   and the modifier class written by hand each time. Centralising prevents the two
   surfaces from drifting on what counts as "gold" (Leaderboard used #1F947, Challenge
   had its own copy of the same code point, and the difference would only show up in a
   regression that would never be caught).

   Challenge was removed on 2026-09-10, so Leaderboard is now the only caller and
   `leaderboard` is the only block prefix actually passed. The parameter is kept because it
   still documents the contract — the modifier class is namespaced per surface, and nothing
   here should ever reach for one global rule across two different scopes.

   ── The glyphs, and why they are named constants ─────────────────────────────
   These were 🍇 / 48 / 49 for a long time, with `// 🥇 🥈 🥉` written beside them.
   That range is U+1F347–1F349 — GRAPES, MELON, WATERMELON — so the top three rows of the
   leaderboard were labelled with fruit, in every theme, on every viewport. The medals are
   U+1F947–1F949, which is a different LEADING surrogate (\uD83E, not \uD83C).

   The exact failure the comment above predicted, then. It survived because a surrogate pair
   is unreadable by eye and the trailing halves differ by one hex digit, so the code looked
   right next to a comment that said what it was meant to be. Naming them removes the pair
   from the call sites entirely; if one is ever wrong again it is wrong in one place, and the
   escape sits next to the literal character in the same line. *@

@code {
    // U+1F947 1st place medal. Written as the escape plus the literal so the two can be
    // checked against each other without decoding a surrogate pair by hand.
    private const string GoldMedal = "🥇";     // 🥇
    private const string SilverMedal = "🥈";   // 🥈
    private const string BronzeMedal = "🥉";   // 🥉

    /// <summary>
    /// Renders the medal for a row whose ordinal rank is <paramref name="rank"/>. Anything
    /// past #3 returns a plain numeric span with no class.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="rank">One-based ordinal — the displayed rank, not the array index.</param>
    /// <param name="blockPrefix">
    /// The BEM block name without the trailing underscore (e.g. <c>leaderboard</c> or
    /// <c>challenge</c>). Used to build the per-surface modifier class.
    /// </param>
    public static RenderFragment Render(int rank, string blockPrefix) => builder =>
    {
        var (glyph, modifier) = rank switch
        {
            1 => (GoldMedal, $" {blockPrefix}__rank--gold po-glow"),
            2 => (SilverMedal, $" {blockPrefix}__rank--silver"),
            3 => (BronzeMedal, $" {blockPrefix}__rank--bronze"),
            _ => (null, string.Empty),
        };

        builder.OpenElement(0, "span");
        builder.AddAttribute(1, "class", $"{blockPrefix}__rank{modifier}");

        if (glyph is null)
        {
            builder.AddContent(2, rank);
        }
        else
        {
            // The medal is decoration and the rank is the content. A `title` on a span is not
            // reliably announced, so the top three used to be a bare emoji as far as assistive
            // tech was concerned — the one column whose whole job is to say what position this
            // row is in. The glyph is hidden and the ordinal is stated instead.
            builder.AddAttribute(2, "title", $"#{rank} Ranked");
            builder.OpenElement(3, "span");
            builder.AddAttribute(4, "aria-hidden", "true");
            builder.AddContent(5, glyph);
            builder.CloseElement();
            builder.OpenElement(6, "span");
            builder.AddAttribute(7, "class", "visually-hidden");
            builder.AddContent(8, $"Rank {rank}");
            builder.CloseElement();
        }

        builder.CloseElement();
    };
}
